  "milestones": [
    {
      "task": "mine nether_quartz_ore",
      "milestone_id": "mine_nether_quartz_ore",
      "description": "Uses inventory_has to check for 'quartz' (Nether Quartz item) in inventory because mining nether_quartz_ore drops the quartz item, which ends up in the player's inventory. The player starts with no quartz, so any quartz in inventory confirms successful mining of the ore.",
      "rules": [
        {
          "type": "inventory_has",
          "params": {
            "item": "quartz",
            "min_count": 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "task": "mine quartz_block",
      "milestone_id": "mine_quartz_block",
      "description": "Uses inventory_has to check for 'quartz_block' in inventory because mining a quartz_block with a pickaxe drops the block itself, which ends up in the player's inventory. The player starts with no quartz_block in inventory, so any quartz_block present confirms successful mining.",
      "rules": [
        {
          "type": "inventory_has",
          "params": {
            "item": "quartz_block",
            "min_count": 1
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

**Design rationale:**

1. **Task 1 — mine nether_quartz_ore**: I use `inventory_has` with item `quartz` because nether_quartz_ore drops the Nether Quartz item when mined (not the ore block itself, unless Silk Touch is used). The diamond pickaxe provided does not have Silk Touch, so the drop will be `quartz`. This is the most direct and reliable check.

2. **Task 2 — mine quartz_block**: I use `inventory_has` with item `quartz_block` because quartz_block drops itself when mined with any pickaxe. The player starts with no quartz_block in inventory, so detecting one confirms the block was mined.

Both rules follow the preferred mapping: **mine tasks → inventory_has** (mined drop ends up in inventory). No spatial/position rules are needed since these are mining tasks, not navigation tasks.

